{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`rebar show <id>` renders only the edges STORED ON THAT TICKET'S RECORD. For a `blocks` relation that\nis the OUTGOING side. So a ticket that is blocked BY something shows NOTHING about it, and the reader\nhas to already know which other ticket to inspect in order to discover the blocker.\n\nMEASURED on the live store:\n\n    rebar show eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a  ->  deps [('blocks', '8625-7bea-67db-4cdc')]\n    rebar show 0d76-ebbf-ee3a-48ac  ->  deps [('blocks', 'eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a')]\n\nThe real chain is `0d76 --blocks--> eb6e --blocks--> 8625`. But reading eb6e alone, the only visible\nedge points DOWNSTREAM, and eb6e looks unblocked.\n\n## The failure this actually caused, today\n\nAn agent inspected eb6e, saw only the outgoing edge, and concluded \"nothing blocks eb6e — its only dep\nis outgoing.\" On that basis it told the operator the ticket could be planned in parallel, and dispatched\na subagent to decompose and plan-review it. The subagent could never have succeeded: per AGENTS.md,\n`review-plan` on a ticket \"open but still blocked by an unclosed dependency\" FAST-FAILS with no LLM\n(unsigned INDETERMINATE, exit 2), and `claim` refuses too. The dispatch was caught and stood down before\nit spent anything, but only because a human asked a follow-up question about dependency edges.\n\nThe reasoning error was reading one side of a bidirectional relation and concluding from it. The tool\nmade that the DEFAULT reading: the information needed to answer \"is this ticket blocked?\" is definitionally\nnot on the ticket you are looking at.\n\n## Why this is worth fixing rather than learning around\n\n* \"Is this blocked?\" is one of the most common questions asked of `rebar show`, and it is the one\n  question the current output cannot answer.\n* It fails toward FALSE CONFIDENCE. The reader does not see an empty or ambiguous field; they see a\n  populated `deps` list that looks complete, and reasonably conclude the picture is whole.\n* `ready` and `next-batch` know the truth — they correctly withhold a blocked ticket — so the data is\n  available; it is only the per-ticket view that omits it. That inconsistency between two tools in the\n  same CLI is itself a trap: an agent that cross-checks `show` against `ready` gets contradictory\n  impressions and has no obvious reason to trust `ready`.\n* Agents are the primary consumers of `rebar show` in this workflow, and they cannot ask a colleague.\n\n## Approach\n\nRender INCOMING edges alongside outgoing ones in the ticket view, clearly labelled by direction — e.g. a\n`blocked_by` list derived from the reverse index, so \"is this blocked?\" is answerable from a single\n`rebar show`. Whether that is a new field, a decorated `deps` list, or a derived summary is a design\ncall; the requirement is that the blocked-ness of a ticket is visible on the ticket.\n\nConsider whether the same asymmetry affects other relations rendered in `deps` (parent/child are already\nhandled separately via `parent_id`, but `discovered_from`, `duplicates` and `relates_to` have the same\none-sided storage), and whether the fix should be general rather than special-cased to `blocks`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `rebar show <id>` on a ticket blocked by an unclosed dependency makes that blocker visible without\n      the reader needing to inspect the blocking ticket.\n- [x] A test constructs `A --blocks--> B` and asserts the blocker is discoverable from `show B`, failing\n      before the fix.\n- [x] The direction of every rendered relation is unambiguous — a reader can tell \"blocks\" from\n      \"blocked by\" without knowing which record the edge is stored on.\n- [x] It is decided and recorded whether the fix generalises to the other one-sided relations\n      (`discovered_from`, `duplicates`, `relates_to`) or is scoped to `blocks`, with the reasoning.\n- [x] `rebar show` and `ready`/`next-batch` cannot disagree about whether a ticket is blocked; a test\n      pins the two against the same fixture.\n- [x] make lint and make typecheck pass by exit code; the default selection is green."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785865304487826001,"uuid":"1d9a1791-2b2e-451e-ae19-e9607f199f52"}